<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carl Menger</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1840">1840</a>–<a href="/years/1921">1921</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Carl Menger was an Austrian economist whose Principles of Economics (1871) set out the theory of marginal utility, independently of William Stanley Jevons and Léon Walras. A professor at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-vienna">University of Vienna</a> and tutor to Crown Prince Rudolf, he founded the Austrian School of economics, later continued by Eugen von Böhm-Bawerk, Ludwig von Mises and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-hayek">Friedrich Hayek</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carl-menger.jpg" alt="Carl Menger" width="320" height="445" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carl_Menger_Portrait_ONB_cropped.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Carl Menger von Wolfensgrün was born on 28 February 1840 in Neu-Sandez, in the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodomeria">Lodomeria</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a>, now Nowy Sącz in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. His father, Anton Menger, was a lawyer. He studied law at the universities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> and received a doctorate from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagiellonian-university">Jagiellonian University</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>. In the 1860s he reported and analysed market news for the Lemberger Zeitung and then the Wiener Zeitung, and noticed a gap between what classical economics said about prices and what people in markets believed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1867 Menger began a study of political economy that led in 1871 to his Principles of Economics. In 1873, aged 33, he received the chair of economic theory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-vienna">University of Vienna</a>. From 1876 he tutored Archduke Rudolf von <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a>, the crown prince, accompanying him on journeys through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-isles">British Isles</a>; their association lasted until the prince's death in 1889.</p><p>His Investigations into the Method of the Social Sciences (1883) set off a dispute with the historical school. Gustav von Schmoller reviewed it unfavourably and used the term Austrian school as a gibe, and in 1884 Menger answered with The Errors of Historicism in German Economics, opening the Methodenstreit, or methodological debate. His disciples included Eugen von Böhm-Bawerk and Friedrich von Wieser. He headed a commission to reform the Austrian monetary system, and in 1903 he resigned his chair to concentrate on study.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Menger rejected the cost-of-production theory of value held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-smith">Adam Smith</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-ricardo">David Ricardo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a>. His subjective theory of value held that price is determined at the margin and that both sides gain from exchange. Unlike William Jevons, he did not believe that goods provide units of utility; goods are valuable because they serve various uses whose importance differs. He explained money as a development of exchange, in which gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, being especially saleable, came to be a general medium of exchange. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-hayek">Friedrich Hayek</a> wrote that the fundamental ideas of the Austrian school belong fully and wholly to Menger.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Carl Menger” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…intellectual capitals of the Western world, producing the foundations of psychoanalysis (Freud), political Zionism (Herzl), the art of the Secession and Expressionism (Klimt, Schiele, Kokoschka), the Second Viennese School of music (Schoenberg, Berg, Webern), and the Austrian school of economics (<b>Menger</b>), while nurturing thinkers such as Ludwig Wittgenstein.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Nowy Sącz, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.63,20.7&amp;year=1892" aria-label="Nowy Sącz on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5026 435 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Carl Menger"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5476 705h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5476 705h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.63,20.7&amp;year=1892">What was here in 1892? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
